  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/31BF1F">Theorem <span data-tag="31BF1F">6.4.2.1</span>.</a>Giraud's theorem!for $0$-topoi Let $\calX $ be a presentable $0$-category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
There exists a small $0$-category $\calC $ which admits finite limits, a Grothendieck topology on $\calC $, and an equivalence $\calX \rightarrow \Shv _{\leq -1}(\calC )$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
There exists an $\infty $-topos $\calY $ and an equivalence $\calX \rightarrow \tau _{\leq -1} \calY $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calX $ is a $0$-topos. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(4)$"><p>
Colimits in $\calX $ are universal. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
